{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChMAPceAmgfrFjG4od4dT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The opus-medium-2 lineage finished all 5 iterations. Its verdict is **CONDITIONAL**: no P0, one P1 and six P2 findings. All four review dimensions were covered, and the last iteration was a cross-check on correctness.\n\n**The P1 (R1-P1-001):** if someone reports a stuck deep review and also types `/deep:review`, the advisor can still offer a fresh `/deep:review` run instead of `/doctor:deep-loop`.\n- `attachCommand` correctly returns `/doctor:deep-loop` for that prompt.\n- The handler then runs `attachTypedCommand` (`advisor-recommend.ts:434`), which puts the typed `/deep:review` back. It never checks for a stuck run.\n- The Python fallback drops the run row on the same prompt, so the two paths disagree. That is the exact case REQ-020 names.\n- The shared test only checks `attachCommand`, which is why both suites pass. AC-027 was marked Met on that same narrower evidence.\n- **Not confirmed end to end:** the advisor's build output (`runtime/dist`) is missing in this worktree, and building it would have written outside the lineage. The finding rests on reading the code plus running the gate functions directly. It goes away if the doctor row, not the hub, ranks first for this prompt.\n\n**The P2s:**\n- **R1-P2-001:** \"skip the deep review\", \"avoid a deep review\" and \"there is no need for a deep review\" still get the offer, in both the TypeScript and Python copies.\n- **R2-P2-001:** the Python fallback runs its checks on the full prompt with no length cap, and the cost grows with the square of the length. A 92 KB prompt took 11.5 s in the Python gate alone.\n- **R3-P2-001:** the Python brief path drops the `typed` flag (`subprocess.ts:200`). A typed deep command there still shows the A/B/C options that packet 013 removed.\n- **R4-P2-001:** the TypeScript and Python tests check the typed-command case at different layers, so the served output can drift from Python with both suites passing.\n- **R5-P2-001:** \"run a deep loop to review the research loop config\" gets `/deep:research`, because the mode shortcut reads \"research loop\" even though the gate already treated it as the thing being reviewed.\n- **R5-P2-002:** a bare \"deep loop: audit X\" is not treated as a loop request. The spec's rule says naming a deep loop counts, and no decision record explains the narrower behaviour.\n\n**Checked and clean:**\n- Both deep commands only ever ask before running (NFR-S01).\n- Prompts reach subprocesses without a shell.\n- No ticket or packet labels appear in code comments.\n- The TypeScript and Python checks gave identical results on 663 prompt variants (the shared test prompts plus extras, each in six spellings).\n\nThe report, five iteration files, records, strategy and registry are all in the lineage folder. A cleanup check found nothing written outside it.\n\nFANOUT_LINEAGE_COMPLETE:opus-medium-2"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1022,"cache_read_input_tokens":287908,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1022},"output_tokens":737,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"<redacted>","uuid":"<redacted>","timestamp":"2026-11-24T04:59:33.066Z","request_id":"<redacted>"}
